= Ecology — Technical =

== Levels and state variables ==
Ecological inference can target organismal traits, population abundance, community interaction structure or ecosystem stocks and fluxes. These choices require operational definitions of the study unit, spatial extent and observation period.

== Dynamics and inference ==
An example population balance is:

<pre>
population change = births + immigration - deaths - emigration
</pre>

This identity is not itself a prediction model; density dependence, environmental variability, detection error and species interactions require further assumptions. Inferring causality from ecological correlations usually needs controlled comparisons, longitudinal data, perturbation or defensible causal designs.

== Ecosystem integration ==
Biotic and abiotic variables interact through resource supply, disturbance, competition, predation and nutrient exchange. Fluxes must specify units and system boundaries. A food-web edge is not automatically evidence of a direct experimental interaction in every context.

